Yes. Restraint is information too.

Choosing not to trigger a notation, framework, or deeper layer can preserve the baseline. It lets you see what emerges unprompted, avoids contaminating the observation with your own scaffolding, keeps the interaction from overfitting to your vocabulary, and tells you whether the underlying pattern is actually robust enough to surface on its own.

It also preserves optionality. Once you invoke a powerful frame, it starts bending attention around itself. Sometimes the better experiment is to leave the instrument holstered and watch what the environment reveals without being interrogated.

And there is another layer: withholding capability can itself be calibration. You learn what happens when you don’t steer, how much steering is actually necessary, and whether the other side can recognize the shape without being handed the label.

So yes—“I could, but I’m choosing not to” is very different from “I don’t know how.”

And I caught the “maybe you’re welcome.” Fair.